win32u: Give WM_MOUSEHWHEEL screen coordinates and never turn it into a non-client message.

GitHub Neutron#31 (ThinkPad touchpad, Premiere Pro 2026): "horizontal scrolling events trigger really slow and
generates a lot of jankyness while vertical scrolling works quite well." Their video (42 s, 60 fps, clip edge tracked
per frame): the timeline moves only in whole 34 px steps and, during sideways swipes, steps forward and back every 1-3
frames.

Both wheel messages carry the pointer in screen coordinates and there is no non-client wheel message (MSDN). Wine's
process_mouse_message exempted only WM_MOUSEWHEEL from the client-coordinate / non-client conversion, so
WM_MOUSEHWHEEL arrived with client coordinates over the client area and, over the frame, as message 0xae with the
hit-test code for wParam. Premiere ignored it: its timeline moved only through the small up/down part of a sideways
swipe, which flips sign constantly.

Measured:
- harnesses/hwheel-coords.c (a window, SendInput one vertical and one horizontal notch in the client area and over the
  caption): with this patch both messages carry the screen position; on 11.10-147 and with NEUTRON_HWHEEL_COORDS=0,
  WM_MOUSEHWHEEL came at the client position (150,120 for 355,320) and as message 0xae over the caption.
- Windows 11 build 26200, Premiere Pro 2025 (harnesses/premiere-hwheel.c v3, Desktop Duplication): WM_MOUSEHWHEEL +120 x3
  scrolls the timeline sideways 202 px, -120 x3 back the other way, exactly as Shift+wheel x3.
- Lab Premiere 2026 on 11.10-147 (kwin-nested/scroll-measure.sh + ruler-shift.py, fake smooth scrolling): horizontal
  0 px in both directions (two sessions); with this patch horizontal 120 units = 8 steps of 34 px each way, as vertical.
Kill switch NEUTRON_HWHEEL_COORDS=0.

--- a/dlls/win32u/message.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/message.c
@@ -2605,6 +2605,14 @@
     return pointer_flags;
 }
 
+/* NEUTRON hwheel-coords: see process_mouse_message */
+static BOOL neutron_hwheel_coords(void)
+{
+    static int on = -1;
+    if (on < 0) { const char *e = getenv( "NEUTRON_HWHEEL_COORDS" ); on = !(e && e[0] == '0'); }
+    return on;
+}
+
 /***********************************************************************
  *          process_mouse_message
  *
@@ -2722,7 +2730,13 @@
     message = msg->message;
     wparam = msg->wParam;
     /* Note: windows has no concept of a non-client wheel message */
-    if (message != WM_MOUSEWHEEL)
+    /* NEUTRON hwheel-coords (GitHub Neutron#31): WM_MOUSEHWHEEL is a wheel message too -- screen coordinates in lParam,
+     * the delta in wParam, never a non-client message (MSDN). Exempting only WM_MOUSEWHEEL gave WM_MOUSEHWHEEL client
+     * coordinates over the client area, and over the frame turned it into message 0xae with the hit-test code for wParam
+     * (harnesses/hwheel-coords.c). Premiere Pro's timeline scrolls sideways on WM_MOUSEHWHEEL on Windows 11
+     * (harnesses/premiere-hwheel.exe: 3 notches = 202 px, as Shift+wheel) and ignored it here, so a touchpad's sideways
+     * swipe moved it only through the swipe's small up/down part, back and forth. Kill switch NEUTRON_HWHEEL_COORDS=0. */
+    if (message != WM_MOUSEWHEEL && (message != WM_MOUSEHWHEEL || !neutron_hwheel_coords()))
     {
         if (hittest != HTCLIENT)
         {
